'''Kijura''' (Burawi: ''Kijura''), officially the '''Democratic Lordship of Kijura''', is a sovereign state located on the southwestern coast of the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] in [[Southwestern Kaftia]]. Constituted in 1822 as a buffer state between [[Burawa]] and the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]], Kijura comprises a territory of approximately 13,481 square kilometres with a coastline of 54 kilometres on the Aethiopian Ocean. The country is bordered by [[Fayeha]] to the north and [[Burawa]] to the east. Its capital and largest city is [[Jawi]].

Kijura is characterised by a system of [[democratic lordship]] in which the head of state—the [[Lord of Kijura]]—is elected by popular vote to a seven-year term, while the [[National Assembly of Kijura|National Assembly]], a popularly elected legislature of 25 districts, serves as the country's sole legislative chamber. A full member of the [[Adonian Community]], Kijura uses the [[International dollar]] as its currency. With an estimated population of 510,500 as of 2024, Kijura is among the smallest nations in [[Kaftia]] by both area and population.

== History ==

=== Creation as a buffer state (1822) ===

Kijura was constituted in 1822 as an artificial buffer state following the [[Burawi War]] (1811–1822), an eleven-year conflict between the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] and the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]]. The war, fought along the disputed border between the two Burawi kingdoms, had exhausted both sides without producing a decisive military outcome. Peace negotiations mediated by the [[Cardinal of Burawa]] from neutral [[Weli Markos]] produced a settlement under which the contested border region was separated from both kingdoms and constituted as an independent state.

The new state was named Kijura, from the Burawi word meaning "the middle place." [[Lord Cusmaan]], a Galwa noble of distinguished lineage but limited political prospects in Galwa itself, was installed as the first ruler. The arrangement removed a potential political challenger from Galwa while providing Burawa with a buffer against its western rival. Kijura was, however, created for political rather than ethnic purposes; the Kijuran people residing in the territory were placed under a newly constituted government that they had not sought.

=== Stable neutral period (1822–1930s) ===

For over a century following its creation, Kijura maintained a position of neutrality between its larger neighbours. The territory served as a neutral intermediary in disputes between Burawa and Galwa, and the lordship passed through three generations of Cusmaan's descendants. During this period, Kijura remained a predominantly feudal society, with attempts at industrialization in the late 1800s as its neighbours modernised.

A railroad constructed in the 1880s and 1890s connected [[Sago]] in Burawa to [[Hargiesa]], Galwa's capital, via Kijura, with the intention of spurring economic development. The region's sparse population, however, resulted in limited use of the line, and Kijura remained primarily agrarian. The majority of the population was concentrated in the capital [[Jawi]] and two smaller settlements in the mountainous interior.

=== Fascist occupation and liberation (1930s–1945) ===

{{main|Great Adonian War}}

In 1938, the rise of fascist movements in Galwa altered Kijura's position. Galwa, under a fascist government, used Kijura as a staging ground for an invasion of Burawa as part of the [[Sago Campaign]]. Kijura was occupied by Galwa forces, and the ruling lords collaborated with the occupiers pursuant to a secret agreement.

The occupation persisted until 1945, when [[Fayeha]]n separatists collaborated with Burawa to repel Galwa forces. The liberation of Kijura disclosed that the Lords had surrendered the territory to the fascists as part of their collaboration agreement. This revelation discredited the hereditary lordship and established the conditions for democratic reform.

=== Democratic reforms (1945–1948) ===

The restoration of the lordship in 1945 was accompanied by fundamental political changes. Influenced by socialist movements in Burawa, Kijura established the [[National Assembly of Kijura|National Assembly]], a popularly elected legislature representing 25 districts across the country. The position of Lord of Kijura was transformed from a hereditary office to an elected one, with the head of state chosen by popular vote to a seven-year term.

In 1948, following the conclusion of the [[Great Adonian War]], [[Fayeha]] achieved independence from Galwa. The ethnic Fayehans, who had been instrumental in liberating Kijura, secured their own territory. This development redrew Kijura's northern border, replacing Galwa with Fayeha as Kijura's northern neighbour.

=== Post-war development (1948–present) ===

Following the democratic reforms, Kijura confronted a decision regarding its political direction: adoption of a full socialist system, as in Burawa, or allowance of multiple political parties. The country chose the latter course, permitting all types of political parties. This decision initially strained relations with Burawa, which had anticipated a closer ideological alignment.

By the 1980s, the [[Socialist Party of Kijura]] had become the dominant force in the National Assembly, normalising relations with Burawa as Burawa increased its economic influence on the small nation. In 2002, [[2002 Kijuran protests|nationwide protests]] erupted after the elected Lord was discovered to have embezzled state funds; the Lord was subsequently recalled. In 2018, Kijura upgraded its main rail line to [[Jawi-Sago high-speed railway|high-speed rail]], connecting Sago to Jawi with modern infrastructure.

== Geography ==

=== Location and boundaries ===

Kijura is situated on the far southwestern coast of the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] in [[Southwestern Kaftia]]. With an area of approximately 13,481 square kilometres, it is one of the smallest nations on the peninsula. The country is bordered by [[Fayeha]] to the north and [[Burawa]] to the east. Its coastline on the Aethiopian Ocean extends for approximately 54 kilometres.

=== Topography ===

The terrain of Kijura consists of an ocean-facing coast with rolling hills leading to the highland interior. The [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]] begins approximately 30 kilometres inland, though elevations in this western portion of the range remain moderate. The country's highest point is [[Buur Dhagax]] (Burawi: "Rock Mountain"), at approximately 920 metres, located in the northern highlands near the Fayeha border.

[[Lake Cusman]], an endorheic lake of approximately 516 square kilometres, lies in the highland interior approximately 61 kilometres from the coast. Named after the founder Lord Cusmaan, the lake is a significant geographic feature and one of the few endorheic lakes in Southwestern Kaftia.

=== Climate ===

Kijura experiences a climate similar to that of the neighbouring Sago region, with more pronounced rainfall along the coast. The highlands receive substantial precipitation, supporting the country's agricultural sector. The coastal zone experiences a Mediterranean-like climate, while the interior highlands are cooler and wetter.

== Government and politics ==

{{main|Politics of Kijura}}

Kijura is a [[unitary state]] with a system of [[democratic lordship]]. The head of state is the [[Lord of Kijura]], elected by popular vote to a seven-year term. The legislature is the [[National Assembly of Kijura]], consisting of 25 members elected from single-member districts.

The incumbent Lord of Kijura is [[Maxamed Kaahin]], who assumed office following the 2018 general election. The [[Socialist Party of Kijura]] has held a majority in the National Assembly since the 1980s, reflecting Burawa's economic influence on the country.

=== Administrative divisions ===

Kijura is divided into 25 electoral districts for the National Assembly. The principal settlements are the capital [[Jawi]] on the coast, and the two smaller mountain towns of [[Dhagax]] and [[Waraabe]] in the highlands.

== Economy ==

Kijura operates as a {{w|tax haven}} with a small financial services sector, following a model comparable to that of {{w|Andorra}}. The country's nominal GDP is approximately $2.7 billion, with a per capita income of approximately $5,290. The economy is based primarily on agriculture (approximately 50 per cent of GDP), followed by financial services (approximately 40 per cent), and small-scale tourism (approximately 10 per cent). The country also derives revenue from railroad transit fees and port operations.

The main rail line connecting Sago to [[Hargiesa]] (formerly Galwa's capital, now in Fayeha) originally traversed Kijura, providing transit revenue. Following Fayeha's independence in 1948, the line was rerouted to connect to [[Saranka]], Fayeha's new capital. The line was upgraded to [[Jawi-Sago high-speed railway|high-speed rail]] in 2018, significantly reducing travel time between Jawi and Sago.

== Demographics ==

=== Population ===

Kijura has an estimated population of 510,500 as of 2024. A substantial [[Kijuran people|Kijuran]] diaspora resides in neighbouring [[Fayeha]], with approximately one million Kijurans living in that country—roughly twice the population of Kijura itself. This demographic configuration results from the circumstances of Kijura's creation; the territory was constituted as a buffer state for political rather than ethnic reasons, placing Kijuran-inhabited lands under a newly created government that the local population had not sought.

=== Ethnicity and language ===

Approximately 80 per cent of Kijura's population is ethnically [[Kijuran people|Kijuran]], a group of the [[Western Kaftian Tradition]]. Kijurans are related to but distinct from the [[Fayehan people|Fayehans]] of neighbouring Fayeha; both groups share the unique "Paleolithic Remnant" genetic profile characteristic of the Western Tradition. A [[Burawi people|Burawi]] minority of approximately 20 per cent resides in Kijura, concentrated primarily in the eastern regions near the Burawa border.

The official language is [[Burawi language|Burawi]], though Kijuran dialects preserve distinctive phonological and lexical features. The adoption of Burawi as the official language reflects the country's historical connection to the Burawi polities of Galwa and Burawa.

=== Religion ===

The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] is the official religion of Kijura, a status shared with neighbouring Burawa. Approximately 70 per cent of the population adheres to this faith. The country's constitution guarantees religious freedom to all residents.

== Infrastructure ==

=== Transportation ===

Kijura's transportation infrastructure is centred on the main rail line connecting [[Sago]] to [[Saranka]], [[Fayeha]]'s capital, via [[Jawi]]. This line was upgraded to [[high-speed rail]] in 2018, reducing travel time and increasing transit capacity. The [[Jawi port]] on the Aethiopian Ocean handles maritime traffic, though the country's limited coastline restricts port operations.

Kijura has no airport; travellers are required to fly via Sago in Burawa. Road connections link Kijura to both Fayeha and Burawa.

== Culture ==

{{main|Culture of Kijura}}

Kijuran culture forms part of the [[Western Kaftian Tradition]], historically characterised by matrilineal social structures and a megalithic building tradition. The Kijuran people retain a distinct cultural identity separate from both the Fayehans and the Burawi, with centuries of political separation having produced unique cultural developments.

Traditional Kijuran folk culture includes the [[Wada-jabad]], a communal dance performed at celebrations and festivals. [[Qalabka painting]], a traditional art form using locally sourced mineral pigments, remains practised in the highland settlements.

The Kijuran diaspora in Fayeha maintains close cultural and economic ties with Kijura, and cross-border family connections are common.

== Military ==

Kijura maintains a paramilitary force under the authority of the national police, supplemented by voluntary reserves. The country has no standing professional military. Security guarantees from both [[Burawa]] and [[Fayeha]] provide for Kijura's external defence, reflecting the country's historic role as a neutral buffer state.

== Education and healthcare ==

Kijura provides universal healthcare to all residents, funded through the national treasury. Education is free through university level, with primary and secondary schooling universally accessible. The country's small population facilitates personalised educational attention, though specialised programmes necessitate travel to neighbouring countries.

== Foreign relations ==

Kijura maintains friendly relations with both [[Burawa]] and [[Fayeha]]. Relations with Burawa were initially strained following the 1948 decision to permit multiple political parties, but normalised as the Socialist Party gained dominance in Kijuran politics. Kijura is a full member of the [[Adonian Community]] and uses the [[International dollar]] as its currency.

